# The lists of msil/test/Programs/c-testsuite and their format (plan 5.12.9,
# 5.17.14; M23, M24): a malformed or stale list must stop lit, never turn
# into fewer tests, and every entry kind must build its test as specified.
# (1) Inputs/ctest-lists.py unit calls msil_ctest_format directly (python
#     -B: no __pycache__ in the source tree): a list with every entry kind
#     ('<name>', '<name> native <triple>  # <reason>', '<name> diag
#     [<triple>] <text>  # M<n> ...', '<name> exclude <triple>
#     long-double|not-portable  # <text>') parses into Entry tuples; each
#     ListError message, with its '<path>:<line>: ' (the grammar, a bad
#     name, a triple that is not linux-gnu or windows-msvc, a diag text with
#     " \ % $ or `, a diag comment that names no milestone, an exclude word
#     outside the vocabulary, a native or exclude entry without a comment, a
#     name listed twice, and the header lines); split_tree and libc_free on
#     a small tree; expected_text (msil-run.py's transcript rule plus
#     '[exit 0]'); and mode_of of every entry kind on both triples.
# (2) The checks of c-testsuite's lit.local.cfg, in nested lits over a copy
#     of the directory (Inputs/nested-ctest runs the copy's lit.local.cfg)
#     with a fake cache (Inputs/make-fake-corpus.py, then
#     Inputs/ctest-lists.py setup writes the tree free1.c, free2.c and
#     calls.c, libc-free.txt and libc.txt): the consistent lists load and
#     yield one test per name, libc.txt's as libc/<name>; without libc.txt
#     (optional until M26) only libc-free.txt's tests; a listed test without
#     its .c.expected (either list), a name the tree lacks or a test the
#     rule puts into a list that lacks it (either list), a libc.txt name the
#     rule does not match, a libc.txt rule that differs from libc-free.txt's,
#     a name listed twice and a ListError are each a configuration error
#     (lit's exit status 2) that names it. A libc.txt also needs hosted mode
#     and %{msil_hosted_cflags} (Inputs/nested-ctest's msil_ctest_drop takes
#     them away, as a lit.cfg.py without M24's hosted mode would lack them),
#     while a tree without libc.txt loads without them. A 'native <triple>'
#     entry needs the native oracle: the setup case 'native' adds natwin
#     (native windows-msvc) and natlin (native linux-gnu) to libc-free.txt,
#     and a nested run (-a) of the one for the configured triple alone
#     (--filter) reports it UNSUPPORTED for the missing feature
#     msil-native-oracle and builds nothing (Inputs/nested-ctest never has
#     the feature: %{harness_lit} does not pass msil_native_oracle on).
# (3) The format's tests executed (prog/Inputs/nested-ctest runs the copy's
#     lit.local.cfg below prog/lit.local.cfg, so %{msil_prog} and
#     %{msil_prog_fail_<L>} build with the stand-ins of
#     prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py, whose compiler accepts only c-testsuite's
#     flags there, and whose prog-stdout markers make the program print the
#     .c.expected text) at -O0 and -O2 on windows-msvc:
#     - plain entries: the MSIL build (and the native build of the native
#       oracle) gets --cflags='-std=gnu17 -w', each compile has those flags,
#       and the MSIL and native runs compare with %t/<name>.expected, which
#       the format wrote from <name>.c.expected (free1: a line; nonl: no
#       final newline, so the file has msil-run.py's '[no newline at end of
#       output]' line; crlf: CRLF folded to LF) and '[exit 0]'; out1, whose
#       program prints nothing, fails with the diff of its text;
#     - nat1 (native windows-msvc): before %{msil_prog}, msil-prog.py
#       --mode native at the first level into %t.expected.d and msil-run.py
#       --native --out %t/nat1.expected write the native transcript, which
#       the test then matches although nat1.c.expected holds another text;
#       nat2 (native linux-gnu) is a plain test here and fails on that text;
#     - diag1 (diag, no triple) runs 'not' + the build piped into FileCheck
#       of the copy's diag.check with -DDIAG=<text> and both
#       --implicit-check-not texts at each level and passes; diag2, whose
#       build succeeds, fails; diag3 (diag linux-gnu) is a plain test here;
#     - exc1 (exclude windows-msvc not-portable) is UNSUPPORTED with
#       'excluded on windows-msvc: not-portable (<text>)'; exc2 (exclude
#       linux-gnu) is a plain test here;
#     - libc.txt's lib1 is 'libc/lib1', compiled with -std=gnu17 -w and
#       %{msil_hosted_cflags} (empty for windows-msvc), with the hosted
#       native oracle (msil-prog.py --mode native --hosted, which compiles
#       with -fms-runtime-lib=dll); lib2 (native windows-msvc) writes its
#       expected file through --mode native --hosted.
#     Under msil_pipeline=lto the plain entries pass and the diag entries
#     are UNSUPPORTED (they require msil-prog-pipeline=obj).

# (1)
# RUN: %python -B %S/Inputs/ctest-lists.py unit > %t.unit.log 2>&1
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.unit.log "^ctest-lists: 38 checks, 0 failed$" "^ok: a list with every entry kind: " "^ok: the grammar \('00001 native'\): " "^ok: a bad test name \('a/b'\): " "^ok: a diag entry's triple that is not one of the lists: " "^ok: a diag text with a character that lit or the checker would change \('error: 100%'\): " "^ok: a diag entry that names no milestone \('  # P3-B variadic'\): " "^ok: an exclude word outside the vocabulary: " "^ok: a native entry without a comment \('00002 native windows-msvc'\): " "^ok: a exclude entry without a comment " "^ok: a name listed twice: " "^ok: split_tree: " "^ok: expected_text\(b'x'\): " "^ok: mode_of with msil-triple-windows: " "^ok: mode_of with msil-triple-linux: "
# RUN: %{harness_check} lacks %t.unit.log "^FAIL: "

